d supplies above 14,000 and up to 15,000 euros, just under the legal limit. If those amounts were spread as in the band immediately below, 7.4 contracts would be expected. The observed-to-expected ratio is 7.2, with a 95% confidence interval of 4.6 to 11.2. December accounts for 2% of the contracts just under the limit, compared with 10% of all its services and supplies contracts. The awardee with the most contracts just under the limit, called awardee A here, has 9. Contract [1], for 14,500 euros, was awarded in October 2025.\nWhat it cannot show: The concentration of contracts near the limit threshold does not indicate improper conduct. It could reflect market pricing, budget constraints, or multiple separate needs. Statistical patterns alone do not prove wrongdoing in procurement processes.\nWhat can be checked: Check if multiple contracts cover the same service or supply to ensure no duplication. Review awardee A's contracts to see if they consistently meet the same need. Examine contract [1] to confirm if the amount was within budget and if the service was necessary.","origen_es":"modelo","origen_en":"modelo","modelo":"Qwen3-4B (Q4_K_M, llama.cpp)","regla":"ss","citas":["EsXeyKKqKceLAncw3qdZkA%3D%3D","6wlQRQxOy9lJ8Trn0ZPzLw%3D%3D","86ZUbxbCCOtLAIVZdUs8KA%3D%3D"],"generado":"2026-11-15"}}